# The three things a board can differ in: the PCB revision, the pixel clock and
# the orientation. Everything else is the module and the panel, which do not vary.
#
# **The default is the board Elecrow sells now.** V1.3 and later is the default. The
# revisions differ only in the backlight bytes the STC8 is sent, and the firmware cannot
# read the revision back, so a build for the wrong revision is wrong without a sign.

menu "Relay display"

choice RELAY_BOARD_REV
    prompt "Board revision"
    default RELAY_BOARD_ADV_V13
    help
        The CrowPanel Advance 7.0 (DIS02170A) revision the build is for. The two differ in
        one thing the firmware acts on: the bytes the STC8H1K28 microcontroller is sent to
        switch the backlight to full brightness and to off. V1.2 uses different values
        from V1.3, V1.4 and V1.5, which is what Elecrow sells now.

        **V1.2 and V1.3 or later cannot be told apart in software.** The STC8 takes any byte
        it is sent and does not answer with its revision, so nothing here can read it. A
        build for the wrong revision sends bytes its board may not use, and no log says so.
        Choose the board's revision when the image is built, and check the backlight on the
        bench (README.md, "The board").

    config RELAY_BOARD_ADV_V12
        bool "V1.2"

    config RELAY_BOARD_ADV_V13
        bool "V1.3, V1.4 or V1.5"
endchoice

config RELAY_PCLK_HZ
    int "Pixel clock (Hz)"
    range 15000000 50000000
    default 16000000
    help
        The RGB pixel clock. Elecrow's examples for V1.3 and later run 16 MHz in their
        ESP-IDF code, which this firmware follows, and 18 MHz in their PlatformIO project.
        Their V1.2 examples run 21 MHz. The default is the ESP-IDF value.

        At 16 MHz a frame is 820 by 500 clocks, so the panel refreshes about 39 times a
        second. A picture that flickers or breaks up on the bench is the first thing to
        check. README.md lists what to look for and which value to try next.

config RELAY_BOUNCE_LINES
    int "RGB bounce buffer (lines, 0 = off)"
    range 0 48
    default 10
    help
        The RGB driver reads the frame from PSRAM as the panel scans. Wi-Fi, flash and
        the CPU share that bus, and when a read is late the picture shifts sideways
        and stays shifted until the next restart of the panel. A bounce buffer is two
        strips of this many lines in internal RAM: the driver fills them from the frame
        buffer ahead of the scan, so a late PSRAM read costs a strip and not the whole
        picture. 10 lines is Elecrow's own ESP-IDF value for this panel. Each line is
        1600 bytes, twice. The count must divide 480 evenly.

config RELAY_DEV_DIAG
    bool "Development diagnostics (timing and task logs)"
    default n
    help
        **A bench build, never a shipped one.** Logs each setup-page connection's life
        and each request's phases in milliseconds, and every five seconds the heap and
        FreeRTOS's per-task CPU share. `sdkconfig.dev` turns it on.

choice RELAY_ROTATION
    prompt "Panel orientation"
    default RELAY_ROTATION_0
    help
        How the panel is mounted. This is the starting value of the stored rotation
        setting, which the device's own page can change later. For 180
        degrees the driver mirrors in both axes, so the frame is drawn the same way
        in either orientation.

    config RELAY_ROTATION_0
        bool "Upright"

    config RELAY_ROTATION_180
        bool "Upside down (mirrored in x and y)"
endchoice

config RELAY_ROTATION_DEG
    int
    default 180 if RELAY_ROTATION_180
    default 0

config RELAY_QEMU
    bool "Build for qemu-system-xtensa (emulator suite)"
    default n
    help
        **A test build, never a shipped one.** It swaps the two things
        qemu-system-xtensa does not model and leaves everything else as the
        board builds it.

        The panel is created with Espressif's virtual RGB panel
        (esp_lcd_qemu_rgb, the managed component) in place of the RGB driver.
        The virtual panel has no mirror, no frame-complete event and no pixel
        clock, so the 180 degree turn is done in software on the way into each
        strip. The backlight calls do nothing, because the emulator has no STC8 to send
        them to, and the boot line still says the backlight is on.
        The frame buffer the virtual panel reads is its own, not PSRAM.

        Wi-Fi is not started. The decision the board would make is logged,
        with a fixed line saying the radio is skipped, because the emulator has
        no radio model.

        Turned on by `sdkconfig.qemu`, which `build.sh --qemu` applies on top of
        `sdkconfig.defaults`. `tests/firmware_compact` reads the image it makes.
        Off by default, and the managed component is only fetched when this is
        on (`main/idf_component.yml`).

endmenu
